  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions & Synoptic Overview:\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery early this morning show upper-\nlevel ridging across the western US and troughing pushing south\nthrough the Great Lakes region and into the upper Ohio River\nValley, while a tropical disturbance sits off the Gulf Coast. At\nthe surface, high pressure is building over the Northern Plains\nin the wake of a cold front that is now south of the Red River\nValley. Missouri and Kansas sit within a region of northwest\nflow, with clear skies early this morning. To the northwest of\nthe region, showers and storms are forming across Nebraska,\nslowly drifting towards northwest Missouri and northeast Kansas.\n\n\nTemperatures/Heat Indices:\n\nMuch cooler, drier air has settled into the area, and will\nremain in place through the end of the work week. While there is\nstill some notable spread between NBM members for temperatures\non Thursday and Friday due to cloud cover and influence from\nmultiple rounds of potential showers and storms, all guidance \npoints towards much drier air remaining in place with \ntemperatures between upper 70s and mid-80s all three days.\n\nGuidance is in agreement that temperatures go back up this \nweekend, with a notable upward trend beginning Saturday pushing \ntemperatures back into the mid-to-upper 90s Saturday-Monday. CPC\nhas 70-90% confidence in above normal temperatures continuing \nthrough the last week of July, and global ensembles have 55-65% \nchances for exceeding heat indices of 105 degrees on Sunday and \nMonday in and around Kansas City as southerly winds advect soupy\ntropical moisture into the area in the low-levels. Enjoy the \ncooler, drier weather while it lasts!\n\n\nRain Chances Today/Thursday:\n\nThe aforementioned showers and storms developing across Nebraska\nearly Wednesday morning are forming along the northern periphery\nof the 700mb high pressure, with broad isentropic ascent as \nwell as pockets of CVA being advected within the westerly flow\non the north side of that circulation. As this activity slowly\nspreads across eastern Nebraska, there is a low chance (<10%) that\nfar northwestern portions of Missouri and northeast Kansas get \nclipped by some decaying showers this morning into early this \nafternoon. Many CAMs depict at least some light showery activity\non reflectivity, but sounding analysis shows that there is a\ndeep layer of dry air above the surface that will need to be\novercome for precip to reach the ground, so if activity does\npersist as far south as our area, it would most likely begin as\nvirga showers that don't reach the ground. That being said, the\nRAP does show a compact vort max/embedded shortwave moving \nthrough the flow and pushing into far northern Missouri by this\nafternoon, so better forcing may allow the dry air to be\novercome this afternoon and some scattered showers to reach the\nground. \n\nAnother shortwave is progged to move through the flow into the\nCentral Plains tonight, with showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping in Kansas and Nebraska late and bringing precip\nchances early Thursday morning. Rain chances begin increasing\njust before sunrise, with the highest rain chances (30-60%) confined\nto areas along and west of I-49/I-29 during the morning hours \non Thursday. The complex of showers and storms that develop is \nexpected to break apart into the afternoon hours on Thursday, \nwith some areas seeing remnant scattered showers through the \nafternoon but with overall coverage decreasing with time.\n\n\nSmoke:\n\nHazy conditions have set into the area, with the northwest flow\naloft continuing to propel smoke into the area before slowly\nmoving it southeast out of the area this afternoon/evening.\nThe nocturnal inversion may trap a marginal amount of smoke near\nthe surface, but significant visibility reductions are not\nexpected in association with this activity, mostly just hazy\nhorizons and potentially a faint smell of smoke early this\nmorning before mixing begins. Any rain that occurs would act to\nbring the smoke particles down to the surface, maybe leaving your\ncar a little dirty like the pollen does when it rains in the\nspring, but rain would also help to clear the smoke pollutants\nout of the atmospheric column and clear the haze out faster. \n\n\nHeavy Rainfall Possible Thursday and Especially Friday:\n\nWhile localized areas of efficient/heavy rainfall will be\npossible on Thursday, Friday appears to be a more conducive day \nfor widespread measurable rainfall and flash flooding. A\nstronger shortwave moves through the Plains and into Missouri\naround daybreak on Friday, with the MCS most likely to develop\nupstream of us along the nose of the LLJ and reach maturity as\nit moves into our forecast area. NAM and GFS show an increasing\nplume of IVT moving into the area during the overnight hours, \nwith the best moisture transport potentially occurring during \nthe daytime hours on Friday. Model soundings continue to \nindicate efficient warm rain processes will dominate with 2+ \nin",
